free-ad5x - plan
================

Goal
----
Open up the FlashForge AD5X: replace FlashForge's closed programs with
open-source ones. FlashForge's Linux stays as it is - no custom distro.

The closed program to replace is firmwareExe (/usr/prog/PROGRAM/software/).
It is the screen app, but it also runs most of the printer.

End goal: one OS for all FlashForge printers. Track OpenCreator
(FlashForge-C5-Modding-Group/opencreator-fs, Creator 5, MIT) as closely as
possible so the two can be merged later.
Last checked: commit 5be3277620 (2026-10-10).
Same idea, different so far:
  ours                         theirs
  startup.sh core + startup.d  firmwareExe script (Wi-Fi + watchdog) ->
    (sequential, once/boot)      startup.sh loop -> scripts/ (parallel)
  - (NaN2008 builds, no NaN   01-fixnan (0x00B330D1, C5 2.0.7, no checks)
    byte; 10-nan-relaxed
    removed 2026-10-17)
  clock in core (ntpclient)    02-fixtime (rdate)
  40-moonraker (/usr/data)     20-msmr (/usr/prog moonrakerDaemon + nginx)
  45-nginx (/usr/data)         20-msmr
  70-grumpyscreen (grumpy-ff)  -
  -                            05-entfix (Entware /opt), 10-klipper,
                               30-camera, 99 updater, tunneled bridge
  eboard wake (AD5X only)      -
  Ethernet/Wi-Fi/hotspot/      Wi-Fi only
    static, cable watch
Worth taking from them: Wi-Fi watchdog (USB reset), camera script,
power_loss_recovery (their klipper-c5).
GrumpyScreen: their installgrumpy.sh downloads pellcorp's release tag
  "main" (legacy NaN, no calibration). Ours: grumpy-ff build (NaN2008,
  GUPPY_CALIBRATE) - see Touchscreen (6).

What firmwareExe does (from firmwareExe 3.1.0)
-----------------------------------------------

Must replace - printer does not work without these:
  1. Starts Klipper at boot (/usr/prog/klipper/start.sh)
  2. Wakes the toolhead board at power-on (sends 'A' on /dev/ttyS5)
  3. Runs printing: start / pause / resume / cancel, via Klipper's API socket
  4. Network: brings up eth0 and Wi-Fi, saves Wi-Fi networks, hotspot mode
  5. Power button and safe power-off
  6. Touchscreen: all menus, screen sleep

Replace if used:
  7. IFS 4-colour unit (/dev/ttyS4): load, unload, colour change, cut, purge
  8. Load cell weighing / crash detection (/dev/ttyS7)
  9. Camera stream (mjpg_streamer, port 8080)
 10. USB stick detect and print
 11. Buzzer sounds
 12. Clock from the internet (ntpclient)

FlashForge-only - drop:
 13. FlashForge cloud (MQTT, Alibaba OSS uploads)
 14. FlashPrint / Orca network printing (Moonraker covers normal slicers)
 15. FlashForge update system
 16. FlashForge calibration / setup wizards

Network (4) - all modes
-----------------------
Settings: "network" in /usr/prog/config/Adventurer5M.json.
Saved Wi-Fi networks: /usr/prog/wifi/wpa_supplicant.conf.
Only one mode is active. Checked in this order at boot:

  Hotspot   wifiHotspotStatus  ifconfig wlan0 up; ifconfig wlan0 192.168.0.240
                               udhcpd -I 192.168.0.240
                               hostapd -B /usr/prog/wifi/rtl_hostapd_2G.conf
  Wi-Fi     wifiStationStatus  wpa_supplicant -d -Dnl80211 -iwlan0
                                 -c/usr/prog/wifi/wpa_supplicant.conf -B
                               wpa_cli -i wlan0 enable_network all
                               then address on wlan0
  Ethernet  ethernetStatus     ifconfig eth0 up; cable = /sys/class/net/eth0/carrier
                               then address on eth0

Address (Wi-Fi or Ethernet):
  isUdhcpc  udhcpc -i <iface>
  isManual  ifconfig <iface> <configIp> netmask <configNetmask>
            route add default gw <configGateway>
            "nameserver <configDns>" in /etc/resolv.conf

Ethernet cable plugged in: killall udhcpc, flush address, DHCP again.
Ethernet cable pulled: killall udhcpc, flush address.
eth0 MAC is set earlier by app_startup.sh from /usr/prog/MAC.
Adding / saving Wi-Fi networks (wpa_cli add_network, save_config) is a
touchscreen menu job (6), not startup.

Touchscreen (6) - GrumpyScreen
------------------------------
Build: FlashForge-C5-Modding-Group/grumpy-ff (2026-10-17). GitHub Actions
checks pellcorp/grumpyscreen's latest release every 6 hours (or by hand,
optional tag and force rebuild), builds that exact commit unchanged and
releases <tag>-ff with grumpyscreen-ff.tar.gz. Build logs: branch
build-logs (latest.log + one file per run).
  - toolchain: musl-cross-make, GCC 12.4, musl 1.2.4, defaults
    mips32r2 / NaN2008 / FP64 / odd-spreg = same ABI as FlashForge's own
    binaries (readelf on firmwareExe and /lib/libc.so.6: flags 0x70001405,
    FP ABI 64-bit FPU, glibc /lib/ld-linux-mipsn8.so.1)
  - static, built with GUPPY_CALIBRATE (on-screen touch calibration)
  - build fails unless readelf shows static, mips32r2, nan2008, FP64
  - release: grumpyscreen, pellcorp's default grumpyscreen.cfg
    (unchanged), start.sh, VERSION (pellcorp tag + commit), root:root
start.sh uses the input device with "Touchscreen" in its name (same rule
as FlashForge's app_startup.sh) and passes it in LVGL_EVDEV_DEV - the
event number depends on driver load order. On the AD5X now: event0 =
md-gpio-keys, event1 = TSC2007 Touchscreen.

Installed (2026-10-17): /usr/data/grumpyscreen (v2.0.0-323-ff), started
by startup.d/70-grumpyscreen.sh with DISPLAY_ROTATE=0 (pellcorp's default
display_rotate: 3 is for the K1 and is sideways here).
Log: /usr/data/logs/grumpyscreen.log.
Touch driver is stock (app_startup.sh insmod: actrue 0-4096, y_flip=1).
Calibration done on screen on first start, saved in
/usr/data/grumpyscreen/calibration.json. Upright, touch working.
Update: download the new release, gunzip -c | tar -xf - -C /usr/data
(calibration.json is not in the tarball, so it is kept).
Power-cycle test (2026-10-17): passed with the legacy-NaN byte at 0x00
(10-nan-relaxed removed) - the build does not need it.

Screen facts: /dev/fb0 32 bpp, virtual 800x960 (two 800x480 buffers),
fb rotate 0, backlight /sys/class/backlight/backlight_gpio0 (on/off only).
CPU: Ingenic X2600 (XBurst II), isa mips32r2/r5, MSA.

History (replaced by grumpy-ff): pellcorp's release is MIPS32r1, FPXX,
legacy NaN - the X2600 kernel (no ieee754= on the command line) refuses
it. 2026-10-11 workarounds: 1-byte e_flags NaN2008 patch; kernel byte
busybox devmem 0x00a130d1 8 1 (kernel 2.0.1; C5: 2.0.5/2.0.6 0x00a130d1,
2.0.7 0x00b330d1) as startup.d/10-nan-relaxed.sh; touch scale in the
tsc2007 actrue values. All dropped.

Power key / power-off (5)
-------------------------
AD5X only has the hard power switch. firmwareExe's power-key code is
shared with other FlashForge models:
  MainWindow::powerKeyChecking finds the input device named
  "md-gpio-keys" (keyboard_gpio_add.ko, PB11, KEY_POWER2) and starts
  PowerKeyThread on it. A key press -> MainWindow::doPowerOff():
  wake screen, "Do you want to turn off the printer ?", Yes ->
  gpio_out(44, 0).
Nothing is wired to PB11 on the AD5X, so this never fires. The same
GPIO 44 = 0 is used by the screen's power-off, power off after print
(printCompletedPowerOff) and FlashForge's app (doPowerOff(string,int)).
What GPIO 44 does on the AD5X is unknown. Optional, not needed for
printing.

Not needed:
 - /dev/ttyS3: FF laziness - unused air-quality sensor code from another
   model; firmwareExe never starts it.

Steps
-----
 1. [done] Startup script that does 1, 2 and 4 instead of firmwareExe.
 2. [part] Moonraker + web UI done. Klipper is still FlashForge's.
 3. [not needed] Power button / power-off (5) - see below.
 4. [done] Open-source touchscreen app (6) - GrumpyScreen (grumpy-ff).
 5. [next] IFS, load cell, camera, USB, buzzer (7-11) as needed.
    Clock (12) done.
 6. [done] Stop firmwareExe from starting.

Printer state (2026-10-10)
--------------------------
Stock FlashForge AD5X firmware (software 3.1.0, control 1.1.1, kernel
2.0.1, library 2.0.1), Zmod removed, all Zmod leftovers wiped.
SSH: root / root (Zmod's AD5X-root.tgz package, run from USB).

Changed on the printer:
  /usr/prog/app_startup.sh      firmwareExe start + 10 s restart block
                                replaced by:
                                /usr/prog/openad5x/startup.sh
                                  > /usr/data/logs/openad5x.log 2>&1 &
  /usr/prog/openad5x/startup.sh copy of startup.sh in this folder
  /usr/data/openad5x/startup.d/ copy of startup.d/ in this folder
  /usr/data/moonraker           Moonraker (moonraker-ad5x.tar.gz)
  /usr/data/moonraker-env       Moonraker's Python packages + libs
  /usr/data/config/moonraker.conf
  /usr/data/nginx               nginx + nginx.conf (fluidd-ad5x.tar.gz)
  /usr/data/fluidd              Fluidd v1.37.6
  /usr/data/tmp                 nginx temp files
  /usr/data/grumpyscreen        GrumpyScreen (grumpy-ff release)

startup.sh order: eboard -> Klipper -> network -> clock -> startup.d
(40-moonraker, 45-nginx, 70-grumpyscreen).
Logs: /usr/data/logs/openad5x.log, openad5x/<drop-in>.log,
      moonraker.log, moonraker.out, nginx-error.log, grumpyscreen.log.

Startup design (installed 2026-10-11, power-cycle test passed)
---------------------------------------------------------------
Core + drop-ins (mix of our startup.sh and OpenCreator's LoopScript):
  startup.sh   core, fixed order: eboard -> Klipper -> network ->
               clock (background) -> startup.d
  startup.d/   optional parts, *.sh run one after another in name order
               (not in parallel like LoopScript). Each script returns
               quickly and backgrounds its own long-running program.
               Rename to .off to disable. A failing script does not stop
               the others. Empty folder / spaces in names are fine.
    40-moonraker.sh    was moonraker_start in startup.sh
    45-nginx.sh        was nginx_start in startup.sh
    70-grumpyscreen.sh GrumpyScreen, DISPLAY_ROTATE=0 (2026-10-17)
    later: 50-usb-automount.sh
Printer paths after the switch (rename free-ad5x -> openad5x):
  /usr/prog/openad5x/startup.sh
  /usr/data/openad5x/startup.d/
  log  /usr/data/logs/openad5x.log (startup.sh, one line per drop-in
       with its exit code), /usr/data/logs/openad5x/<name>.log per drop-in
  app_startup.sh line becomes:
    /usr/prog/openad5x/startup.sh > /usr/data/logs/openad5x.log 2>&1 &

Web: http://<printer>/ (Fluidd via nginx), Moonraker on port 7125.

Backups are kept off the printer: original app_startup.sh (= official
3.1.0 file, md5 08ba2971...) is on the 31 GB USB stick in
free-ad5x-backup/.

Moonraker package (moonraker-ad5x.tar.gz)
-----------------------------------------
Moonraker commit abd2026b90 - same commit Simple AF pins. Later commits
bump the Python requirements (3ea83163b0, paho-mqtt 2.1.0 etc.), which
Simple AF pinned against. Reports version v0.9.3-abd2026.
Runs on FlashForge's Python 3.8.2:
  PYTHONPATH      /usr/data/moonraker-env/site-packages
  LD_LIBRARY_PATH /usr/data/moonraker-env/lib + FlashForge libs
  SSL_CERT_FILE   certifi bundle in site-packages
site-packages: pure-Python packages from Simple AF's k1/moonraker-env.tar.gz
  (pip/setuptools/virtualenv left out).
From Creality Ender-3 V3 firmware F001_V1.2.3.23 (same MIPS NaN2008/FP64
  ABI): sqlite3 package, _sqlite3 module, libsqlite3.so.0, libsodium.so.23.
From FlashForge's Python: lmdb, pillow 7.0.0, streaming-form-data 1.8.1,
  pyserial.
No [update_manager] in moonraker.conf - Moonraker must not update itself.

Web package (fluidd-ad5x.tar.gz)
--------------------------------
nginx 1.17.7 from the same Ender-3 V3 firmware (needs libpcre.so.1,
libz.so.1 - both on the AD5X). Port 80 serves /usr/data/fluidd and passes
/websocket, /printer, /api, /access, /machine, /server to Moonraker.
Harmless startup alert: nginx tries its built-in /var/log/nginx/error.log
before reading nginx.conf.

Building the packages (on the PC)
---------------------------------
build/moonraker/  build.py, moonraker.conf
build/fluidd/     build.py, nginx.conf

Downloads and extracted files are not kept here. Put them in a throwaway
folder next to a copy of build.py:

moonraker-ad5x.tar.gz
  <folder>/dl/moonraker.tar.gz   https://codeload.github.com/Arksine/
                                 moonraker/tar.gz/<MR_COMMIT in build.py>
  arg 1: Simple AF's k1/moonraker-env.tar.gz (pellcorp/creality)
  arg 2: folder with files extracted from F001_V1.2.3.23.ingenic
         images/rootfs.squashfs (7-Zip): usr/lib/libsqlite3.so.0.8.6,
         usr/lib/libsodium.so.23.3.0, usr/lib/python3.8/sqlite3/*.py,
         usr/lib/python3.8/lib-dynload/_sqlite3.cpython-38-mipsel-linux-gnu.so
  python build.py <moonraker-env.tar.gz> <f001 folder>

fluidd-ad5x.tar.gz
  <folder>/nginx.conf
  <folder>/dl/fluidd.zip         Fluidd release (v1.37.6 used)
  arg 1: same F001 folder, plus usr/sbin/nginx and etc/nginx/mime.types
  python build.py <f001 folder>

Install: stream to the printer with gunzip -c | tar -xf - -C /usr/data
(busybox tar here has no -z). Copy moonraker.conf to
/usr/data/config/moonraker.conf.

Power-cycle test (2026-10-10): passed. startup.sh started eboard, Klipper
(both MCUs configured), network, Moonraker (klippy ready, no failed
components, v0.9.3-abd2026) and nginx (Fluidd on port 80).

Clock (12): no battery clock, boots at 1970. startup.sh runs clock_sync
in the background: /usr/prog/ntpclient -c 1 -s -h pool.ntp.org every 5 s
until the year is >= 2024 (max 5 min), then logs "clock: <date>".
firmwareExe ran it without -c, which leaves ntpclient running as a daemon
(re-sync every 600 s); -c 1 sets the time once and exits.
With a 1970 clock HTTPS fails ("certificate is not yet valid").

Clock power-cycle test (2026-10-11): passed. "clock: Sun Oct 11 01:17:42
UTC 2026" logged, no ntpclient left running, no certificate errors in
moonraker.log.
